WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.432 --> 00:00:11.749
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> This is Charlotte Campbell. She’s twenty-five and she’s a busker.

2
00:00:12.755 --> 00:00:16.900
Charlotte plays the guitar and sings on the South Bank in London.

3
00:00:17.673 --> 00:00:20.947
Today, she’s busking near the London Eye.

4
00:00:21.904 --> 00:00:28.085
<v><b>Charlotte:</b></v> ‘On a typical day when I’m going busking, I get the train to Waterloo and I walk to the London Eye.

5
00:00:28.134 --> 00:00:33.125
I start at about eleven o’clock and I play until one. Then I take a lunch break.

6
00:00:33.248 --> 00:00:39.883
And sometimes I stay until six or seven p.m. but in the winter when it gets dark I go home a little earlier.’

7
00:00:40.631 --> 00:00:43.059
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> You can find buskers everywhere –

8
00:00:43.182 --> 00:00:51.570
in the streets, in shopping centres, outside bars and restaurants and in the parks of most British cities.

9
00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:56.856
In London, you can also listen to music while you’re on the London Underground.

10
00:00:57.138 --> 00:01:05.122
Buskers play or sing all kinds of different music – opera, jazz, pop and folk.

11
00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:10.568
<v><b>Charlotte:</b></v> ‘I like music that’s on the radio, Ed Sheeran and things like that,

12
00:01:10.653 --> 00:01:14.308
and some older music from the 60s, like the Beatles.’

13
00:01:15.534 --> 00:01:18.282
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> But busking isn’t always easy.

14
00:01:18.466 --> 00:01:25.885
London is a busy city and there are lots of buskers, which means you can’t just play music wherever you like.

15
00:01:26.204 --> 00:01:30.031
<v><b>Charlotte:</b></v> ‘You need to get a permit to busk in London, and pass an audition,

16
00:01:30.043 --> 00:01:34.446
but when you do, there’s a really nice community and busking in London becomes easy.’

17
00:01:35.476 --> 00:01:41.068
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> So buskers like Charlotte have to choose an area of London to sing and play in.

18
00:01:41.203 --> 00:01:48.083
They share this area with other buskers and sometimes they have to wait for the other buskers to finish.

19
00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:56.153
Charlotte lives in Brixton, in South London.

20
00:01:58.373 --> 00:02:03.009
In her flat, she writes her own songs and practises.

21
00:02:11.262 --> 00:02:14.120
<v><b>Charlotte:</b></v> ‘I love writing songs. I love writing my own music.’

22
00:02:14.427 --> 00:02:19.259
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> For Charlotte, there are good and bad things about life as a busker.

23
00:02:19.479 --> 00:02:23.281
<v><b>Charlotte:</b></v> ‘I like meeting new people and sharing my music with a new audience.

24
00:02:25.808 --> 00:02:27.316
I don’t like it when it gets cold.’

25
00:02:27.316 --> 00:02:29.303
Ba-baa, ba-baa, there’s music in my heart

26
00:02:30.014 --> 00:02:34.785
Ba-baa, ba-baa, there’s music on the streets of London

27
00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.704
That’s where we are, that’s where we are

28
00:02:41.285 --> 00:02:44.817
Ah-hoo, yeah I get lonely

29
00:02:45.381 --> 00:02:48.545
Ah-hoo, yeah I get cold

30
00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:54.015
Ah-hoo, I get tired, oh so tired

31
00:02:54.064 --> 00:02:57.277
But I’ll never sell my soul.

32
00:02:58.614 --> 00:03:02.134
Ah-hoo, from Piccadilly

33
00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:07.248
Ah-hoo, to Leicester Square

34
00:03:07.972 --> 00:03:11.405
Oh I gave my heart to London

35
00:03:12.472 --> 00:03:15.477
And you’ll always find me there.

36
00:03:16.838 --> 00:03:22.014
Ba-baa, ba-baa, there’s music on the streets of London

37
00:03:22.063 --> 00:03:25.840
Ba-baa, ba-baa, there’s music in my heart

38
00:03:26.159 --> 00:03:31.335
Ba-baa, ba-baa, there’s music on the streets of London

39
00:03:31.703 --> 00:03:34.499
That’s where we are, that’s where we are